# Algorithms, Systems & Simulation Projects

A collection of programming projects written by **Paweł Kosek** (Computer Science & Mathematics, University of Warsaw).
They span five languages – **C, C++, Java, x86-64 Assembly and Python** – and range from low-level memory management to graph search and discrete-event simulation.

Most projects were written as university assignments with strict specifications: aggressive compiler flags (`-Wall -Wextra -Werror -Wconversion`, sanitizers), hidden test suites and Valgrind-checked memory safety. Each folder includes the original problem statement where available.

| Project | Language | Key topics |
|---|---|---|
| [Recursive Stacks](#recursive-stacks--c) | C | Reference counting, cycle collection, shared library, exception safety |
| [Water Glasses Solver](#water-glasses-solver--c) | C++ | BFS over a state space, mixed-radix state encoding, number theory |
| [Ski Resort Simulation](#ski-resort-simulation--java) | Java | Discrete-event simulation, OOP design, graph algorithms, unit tests |
| [Discrete Fractals](#discrete-fractals--x86-64-assembly) | x86-64 NASM | Linux syscalls, manual memory management, string rewriting (L-systems) |
| [Medals Counter](#medals-counter--c) | C++ | Input validation with regex, hashing, O(1) multi-criteria queries |
| [SANO Research Report](#sano-research-report--tda-of-proteins) | Python / report | Topological Data Analysis, protein embeddings |

---

## Recursive Stacks – C

📁 [`Recursive_Stacks_C/`](Recursive_Stacks_C)

A dynamically loaded C library (`librstack.so`) implementing **recursive stacks**: stacks whose elements are either `uint64_t` values or references to other stacks. A stack can be pushed onto many stacks at once (it is shared, not copied), and the reference graph **may contain cycles**.

**Highlights**
- **Memory management via reference counting** combined with a **synchronous cycle collector** (trial-deletion algorithm with grey/white/black marking, in the spirit of Bacon & Rajan). When a stack's counter drops, the library detects garbage cycles that pure reference counting would leak and frees them.
- **Strong exception-safety guarantee** for allocation failures: if `malloc` fails mid-operation, the structure is left unchanged and nothing leaks (verified with fault-injecting `malloc` wrappers and Valgrind).
- Recursive queries (`rstack_empty`, `rstack_front`) and file I/O (`rstack_read`, `rstack_write`) that stay correct in the presence of cycles.
- Strict validation of input files (overflow, negative numbers, non-decimal input) with appropriate `errno` values.

**Build**
```bash
cd Recursive_Stacks_C
make            # produces librstack.so
make clean
```

The public interface (`rstack.h`) and the allocation-failure test harness (`memory_tests.c/h`) were provided with the assignment; the implementation is in `rstack.c`.

